WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.130
SPEAKER 1: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.934
featured a rich variety of talks
detailing the future of cloud.

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:07.350
If you didn't get a chance to
catch the "Location-Powered,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.120
On-Demand Economy,"
then stay tuned,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.746
because here's the recap.

00:00:10.746 --> 00:00:12.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.967 --> 00:00:17.050
SPEAKER 2: Powered by smart
phones and the ability

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.690
to determine location,
the on-demand economy

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.210
reflects a significant
shift in user behaviors

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.124
and expectations.

00:00:23.124 --> 00:00:25.540
SPEAKER 1: As a result, we've
seen significant disruptions

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.190
in many traditional businesses.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.890
For example, we used to
hail a taxi on the street.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.980
Now we can request a car
from an app on our phones.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.110
Or consider that eating
restaurant food at home

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:37.430
was limited to take-out
or pizza delivery.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.580
Today, we can get anything
from fresh ingredients

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.220
to meals from just about
any restaurant delivered

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.544
within the hour.

00:00:43.544 --> 00:00:45.460
It's important to note
that these new business

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.430
models are driven by
location-powered technology.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.680
Customers request
cars or place an order

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.560
on their apps that indicate
their current location.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.230
Meanwhile, the fleet of drivers
and delivery people fulfilling

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:59.020
those requests are constantly
using location-powered apps

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.870
to figure out how to
get to their customers.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.180
SPEAKER 2: For example,
traditional taxi and delivery

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.340
drivers had to memorize
routes and locations

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.080
to efficiently
navigate their cities

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.700
without digital assistance.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.220
But with real-time navigation
and routing technology,

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.720
this need for
specialized knowledge

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.960
is removed, enabling businesses
to significantly increase

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.410
their fleet of drivers
with less training cost.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:22.450
Now you can include
those same features

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.890
within your own apps using
the Google Maps APIs, which

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:30.340
are available on Android, iOS,
web, and HTTP-based services.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.830
These APIs give you the
power to do everything

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.830
from visualizing points on a
map to routing and planning

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:39.070
for deliveries to preprogramming
television remotes based

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:40.366
on their physical location.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:41.740
SPEAKER 1: In
order to understand

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.840
how to take best advantage
of these features,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.020
let's focus on user
habits and expectations.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.820
Constant technology
advancements have

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.760
led to the user expectations
of continuous improvements

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.650
in speed and convenience.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.355
We expect our web
pages to load faster,

00:01:55.355 --> 00:01:56.820
our movies to be
higher resolution,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.900
our deliveries to
arrive within minutes,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.690
and our phones to not only tell
us how to reach a destination,

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.810
but also to navigate around
accidents or bad traffic.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.490
In terms of services,
that translates

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.100
into expectations for
continually improving

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.500
simplicity and predictability.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.230
Sadly, one caveat with
this economic trend

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.700
is that while user expectations
for simple and convenient

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.090
services is on the
rise, willingness

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.860
to pay for them
is on the decline.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.920
For example, cheap fixed
price physical deliveries soon

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.960
became unlimited free delivery.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.150
And low prices for
digital goods quickly

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:32.290
evolved into unlimited plans
for music video and mobile data.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.090
With service
expectations increasing

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.347
and consumer willingness
to pay decreasing,

00:02:36.347 --> 00:02:37.930
the challenge for
companies is to find

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.120
ways to deliver
efficiencies of scale that

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.890
will enable the margins
that they need to thrive.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:44.890
SPEAKER 2: The issue with
location-based systems

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.200
is no longer the logistics
in managing a fleet,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.894
deploying it, or even
getting to the last mile.

00:02:49.894 --> 00:02:51.310
Instead, the
challenge has shifted

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:53.050
to the last tenth of a mile.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.580
For example, it's not enough
to just go to an address.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.970
You now have to find a
specific person standing

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:59.920
on the corner of that address.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.050
The result is that the
new location-based economy

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.610
isn't really about location.

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:04.810
It's about places.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.090
The concept of a place
is important to consumers

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:07.717
on the move.

00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:10.300
People want to know that before
they head out to a restaurant,

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:11.260
is it going to be busy?

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.610
Is it open right now?

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.440
Or is it even worth going to?

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.720
While the previous
location-based data changes

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:17.500
slowly--

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.820
months at a time--

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.770
this new location
economy is filled

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.500
with information that's dynamic,
affected by the time of day,

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:28.000
traffic, local events, and even
changes in a business location.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.430
SPEAKER 1: Keeping up with
all of these changes yourself

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.456
globally is really hard, which
is where Google can help.

00:03:34.456 --> 00:03:35.830
The Google Maps
team is committed

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:37.990
to finding creative
solutions to help developers

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:41.590
like you find efficiencies
in the last tenth of a mile.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:44.200
The result is that our Mapping
APIs return up-to-the-minute

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.050
data that reflects the dynamic
nature of the location-powered

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:48.107
economy.

